1
00:00:00,320 --> 00:00:07,840
Right now, a complete stranger is drowning in debt —
so that the money in your account can exist.

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:11,040
That's not a metaphor.

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:16,720
If every debt on Earth were paid off tonight,
your savings would vanish with them.

4
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:23,600
Because every dollar you've ever earned
was born the same way —

5
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:26,400
not printed, not saved: borrowed into existence.

6
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,360
Give me five minutes and you'll see
the trick with your own eyes.

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:36,480
Why prices never stop rising.
Why your savings never feel like enough.

8
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:42,160
And why this machine needs you in debt forever.

9
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,520
No jargon. Just mechanisms.

10
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:49,120
Walk into a bank and borrow $20,000.

11
00:00:49,120 --> 00:00:55,280
Most people think the bank takes $20,000
from someone's savings and hands it to you.

12
00:00:55,280 --> 00:01:02,160
That is not what happens.
Nobody's account goes down. Not one.

13
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:08,960
The banker types 20,000 into a screen —

14
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:15,119
and brand-new money appears.
Money that did not exist one second earlier.

15
00:01:15,119 --> 00:01:18,159
And if that sounds like a conspiracy theory,
here's the fun part.

16
00:01:18,159 --> 00:01:24,720
In 2014, the Bank of England — the world's oldest
central bank — published a paper admitting it in plain English:

17
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:30,240
when a bank lends, it creates the deposit.

18
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:41,360
Around 97% of all money in the economy was made
exactly this way. Not minted — typed.

19
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:43,360
But here's the part they left out of the headline.

20
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:50,320
The bank created your $20,000 —
but you owe back $23,000.

21
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:54,159
So where do the extra $3,000 come from?

22
00:01:54,159 --> 00:01:58,159
The bank never created them. Nobody did.

23
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:04,799
The only place that money can exist
is inside someone else's loan.

24
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:07,920
Think about what that means.

25
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:12,879
Systemwide, the debt is always
bigger than the money. Always.

26
00:02:12,879 --> 00:02:19,280
It's musical chairs with your livelihood — millions of
people scrambling for dollars that don't all exist.

27
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:26,239
And the music only keeps playing
if new borrowers keep joining the game.

28
00:02:26,239 --> 00:02:31,040
More loans, more debt, every single year.

29
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:37,680
Not because we're greedy — because the machine
stalls the moment we stop.

30
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:44,720
So who pays for all this new money? You do —
just never as a bill with your name on it.

31
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:53,120
Every fresh dollar typed into existence quietly
shrinks the ones already in your pocket.

32
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:59,599
That's the inflation you feel at the grocery store.
The tax you never voted for.

33
00:02:59,599 --> 00:03:07,519
And watch where the new money lands first:
banks, funds, asset owners.

34
00:03:07,519 --> 00:03:15,920
They buy before prices rise. Your salary shows up
last — after everything already costs more.

35
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:21,440
And when the machine finally choked in 2008,
did it apologize?

36
00:03:21,440 --> 00:03:29,200
It sent the bill — $700 billion —
to the public that never got a vote.

37
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:33,440
You weren't bailed out. You were billed.

38
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:44,239
Now — what if I told you there's a rule set 1,400 years
old, engineered to make this exact trick impossible?

39
00:03:44,239 --> 00:03:48,720
Three rules. Rule one: money cannot rent money.

40
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:56,159
Charging for the passage of time — Riba — is banned.

41
00:03:56,159 --> 00:03:59,200
No interest, no debt spiral — by design.

42
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:05,439
Rule two: every deal must tie to something real.
A house, a car, a business.

43
00:04:05,439 --> 00:04:12,239
Money stays a measuring stick.
It never becomes the merchandise.

44
00:04:12,239 --> 00:04:15,840
Rule three: risk is shared.

45
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:22,880
The financier profits only when you do —
and eats the losses with you.

46
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:26,479
No more heads-they-win, tails-you-pay.

47
00:04:26,479 --> 00:04:30,400
That's not nostalgia. That's engineering.

48
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,840
You can't opt out of the money machine overnight,

49
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:39,040
but you can stop feeding it blindly.

50
00:04:39,040 --> 00:04:42,479
Know what your savings are actually doing.

51
00:04:42,479 --> 00:04:45,600
Question every contract that rents you money.

52
00:04:45,600 --> 00:04:54,880
And remember the test: if no real asset changed hands,
someone's just charging you for time.

53
00:04:54,880 --> 00:05:04,960
We've decoded every gear of this machine — savings,
mortgages, inflation, insurance — in under 3 minutes each.

54
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:08,560
The full series is below.

55
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:15,440
Next Friday: the mortgage that owns you —
and the contract that flips it.

56
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:17,919
No jargon. Just mechanisms.
